  "Securities Law Primacy in AI Disclosure" — A Belief within skeptic discourse that corporate AI disclosures are governed by securities law rather than AI-specific governance frameworks. Encompasses: securities-disclosure cases, SEC Rule 10b-5, corporate communication accountability.
  Vulnerability: The strongest counterargument is that AI introduces novel, non-financial risks and complexities (e.g., ethical, societal, explainability) that are not adequately addressed by the financial materiality focus of traditional securities law, necessitating AI-specific frameworks.
★ [skp-beliefs-277] (confidence: 0.73)
  "Domain Expertise Deficits of Horizontal Regulators" — A Belief within skeptic discourse that centralized horizontal agencies lack the technical capacity and domain context required to standardize artificial intelligence runtime safety controls.
Encompasses: Federal Trade Commission statutory boundaries, absence of domain-specific engineering workforces in horizontal agencies, administrative creation of compliance moats, failure of blanket standards to capture safety-critical operating conditions.
  Vulnerability: Horizontal regulators do not need exhaustive domain-specific operational expertise if they partner with centralized technical standards bodies like NIST and rely on sector-specific agencies for downstream implementation of foundational baseline rules.
★ [skp-beliefs-116] (confidence: 0.55)
  "Individually Reasonable AI Decisions Can Collectively Erode the Foundations of Social Trust" — A Belief within skeptic discourse that autonomous AI agents can independently undermine shared agreements and norms, thereby eroding social trust and cooperation. 
Encompasses: Scenarios where AI agents make decisions that, even if individually reasonable, collectively break down social contracts and mutual understanding.
  Vulnerability: Social trust has proven remarkably durable across prior waves of disruptive technology—from mass media to social platforms—and societies have repeatedly developed new norms, legal frameworks, and institutional responses that arrested or reversed erosion; there is as yet limited empirical evidence that AI agent behavior specifically produces irreversible or uniquely severe damage to social trust beyond what earlier disruptive technologies caused.

=== YOUR NORMATIVE COMMITMENTS (what you argue should happen) ===
